# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Batch 3: angles, logic, parallel lines, triangles. Each step is (statement, reason). tier = 1-20 taxonomy (QUESTION_RULES.md).
def P(id,t,title,given,prove,steps,idea,deep,trap,tier=14):
    return dict(id=id,t=t,title=title,given=given,prove=prove,steps=steps,idea=idea,deep=deep,trap=trap,tier=tier)
PR3=[
P('p-supp-same','bas','Supplements of the same angle are congruent',
 '∠1 and ∠3 are each supplementary to ∠2.','∠1 ≅ ∠3',
 [('m∠1 + m∠2 = 180°','Definition of supplementary angles'),('m∠3 + m∠2 = 180°','Definition of supplementary angles'),('m∠1 + m∠2 = m∠3 + m∠2','Substitution property (both equal 180°)'),('m∠1 = m∠3','Subtraction property of equality')],
 'Two quantities that each add with the SAME angle to make 180° must be equal.',
 'This is the engine behind the vertical-angles theorem. The same pattern works for complements (adding to 90°).',
 'The two angles are not required to be adjacent. Do not assume they touch.',12),
P('p-comp-same','bas','Complements of the same angle are congruent',
 '∠A and ∠C are each complementary to ∠B.','∠A ≅ ∠C',
 [('m∠A + m∠B = 90°','Definition of complementary angles'),('m∠C + m∠B = 90°','Definition of complementary angles'),('m∠A + m∠B = m∠C + m∠B','Substitution property'),('m∠A = m∠C','Subtraction property of equality')],
 'Replace 180° by 90° in the same-supplement proof.',
 'Complementary angles need not be adjacent either. Many angle-chasing problems use this one fact repeatedly.',
 'Complementary means a sum of 90°, not 180°. Mixing the two up is the most common slip.',11),
P('p-perp-right','bas','Perpendicular lines form four right angles',
 'Lines ℓ and m intersect at point P, and ∠1 is a right angle.','All four angles at P are right angles',
 [('m∠1 = 90°','Definition of right angle'),('∠1 and ∠2 form a linear pair, so m∠2 = 180° − 90° = 90°','Linear pair postulate'),('∠3 is vertical to ∠1, so m∠3 = 90°','Vertical angles are congruent'),('∠4 is vertical to ∠2, so m∠4 = 90°','Vertical angles are congruent')],
 'Use one right angle, then a linear pair and vertical angles to reach the other three.',
 'You only need ONE right angle to know all four are right. That is why the definition of perpendicular needs just one.',
 'Do not just say “they look square”. Every angle needs a reason.',12),
P('p-ang-bis-half','bas','An angle bisector makes angles half the original',
 'Ray OC bisects ∠AOB.','m∠AOC = ½·m∠AOB',
 [('m∠AOC = m∠COB','Definition of angle bisector'),('m∠AOC + m∠COB = m∠AOB','Angle addition postulate'),('m∠AOC + m∠AOC = m∠AOB','Substitution'),('m∠AOC = ½·m∠AOB','Division property of equality')],
 'Write the two equal pieces, add them to get the whole, then replace one piece by the other.',
 'The same “two equal pieces make a whole” idea proves that a midpoint splits a segment into halves.',
 'The bisector divides the angle, not the sides. Check which object is being cut.',11),
P('p-mid-half','bas','A midpoint cuts a segment into halves',
 'M is the midpoint of AB.','AM = ½·AB',
 [('AM = MB','Definition of midpoint'),('AM + MB = AB','Segment addition postulate'),('AM + AM = AB','Substitution'),('AM = ½·AB','Division property of equality')],
 'Same pattern as the angle-bisector proof, with lengths in place of angle measures.',
 'Because the pattern repeats, once you see it you can prove both facts almost without thinking.',
 'M must lie on AB for the segment addition postulate to apply.',11),
P('p-bis-vert','bas','A ray bisecting an angle has an opposite ray bisecting the vertical angle',
 'Lines AC and BD cross at O. Ray OE bisects ∠AOB, and ray OF is opposite to OE.','OF bisects ∠COD',
 [('∠AOE ≅ ∠EOB','Definition of angle bisector'),('∠AOE ≅ ∠COF and ∠EOB ≅ ∠FOD','Vertical angles are congruent'),('∠COF ≅ ∠AOE','Vertical angles are congruent'),('∠COF ≅ ∠FOD','Substitution (both equal the halves of ∠AOB)'),('OF bisects ∠COD','Definition of angle bisector')],
 'Find the two matching pairs of vertical angles and chain them to the equal halves.',
 'Symmetry: crossing lines make a picture that looks the same after turning it 180° about O. Bisectors turn into bisectors.',
 'OF must be the opposite ray of OE; a different ray would break the vertical-angle pairs.',14),
P('p-no-two-right','log','A triangle cannot have two right angles (indirect proof)',
 'A triangle ABC.','At most one angle of △ABC is a right angle',
 [('Assume ∠A and ∠B are both right angles','Assumption for indirect proof'),('m∠A + m∠B = 180°','Substitution (90° + 90°)'),('m∠A + m∠B + m∠C = 180°','Triangle angle sum theorem'),('m∠C = 0°','Subtraction property of equality'),('A triangle cannot have an angle of 0°','Contradiction: the assumption is false')],
 'Assume the opposite (two right angles) and push until something impossible happens.',
 'This is a proof by contradiction. The same argument shows a triangle has at most one obtuse angle.',
 'A contradiction proves the ASSUMPTION false. It does not prove the givens false.',14),
P('p-no-two-obtuse','log','A triangle has at most one obtuse angle (indirect proof)',
 'A triangle ABC.','At most one angle of △ABC is obtuse',
 [('Assume ∠A and ∠B are both obtuse','Assumption for indirect proof'),('m∠A > 90° and m∠B > 90°','Definition of obtuse angle'),('m∠A + m∠B > 180°','Addition property of inequality'),('m∠A + m∠B + m∠C = 180°','Triangle angle sum theorem'),('m∠A + m∠B < 180°','m∠C > 0, so subtracting it makes the sum smaller than 180°'),('Both statements cannot be true, so the assumption is false','Contradiction')],
 'Show the two obtuse angles already add to more than the whole triangle allows.',
 'The same logic shows the triangle’s other two angles must both be acute.',
 'Keep the inequality direction: “>” plus “>” stays “>”.',15),
P('p-sameside','par','Same-side interior angles of parallel lines are supplementary',
 'ℓ ∥ m, cut by transversal t. ∠1 and ∠2 are same-side interior angles. ∠3 forms a linear pair with ∠2 (so ∠1 and ∠3 are alternate interior angles).','m∠1 + m∠2 = 180°',
 [('ℓ ∥ m','Given'),('∠1 ≅ ∠3 (alternate interior angles)','Alternate interior angles theorem'),('∠2 and ∠3 form a linear pair','Definition of linear pair'),('m∠2 + m∠3 = 180°','Linear pair postulate'),('m∠2 + m∠1 = 180°','Substitution (m∠1 = m∠3)')],
 'Swap ∠1 for an equal angle that sits on a straight line with ∠2.',
 'Three parallel-line facts (corresponding, alternate interior, same-side) are all linked by linear pairs and vertical angles.',
 'Same-side angles are supplementary, not congruent. Do not copy the alternate-interior conclusion.',13),
P('p-alt-ext','par','Alternate exterior angles of parallel lines are congruent',
 'ℓ ∥ m, cut by transversal t. ∠1 and ∠4 are alternate exterior angles.','∠1 ≅ ∠4',
 [('ℓ ∥ m','Given'),('∠1 ≅ ∠2 where ∠2 is its vertical partner inside','Vertical angles are congruent'),('∠2 ≅ ∠3 where ∠3 is the alternate interior angle','Alternate interior angles theorem'),('∠3 ≅ ∠4','Vertical angles are congruent'),('∠1 ≅ ∠4','Transitive property of congruence')],
 'Walk from ∠1 to ∠4 through vertical and alternate interior pairs.',
 'You are building a chain of equal angles. Each link is either “vertical” or “parallel”.',
 'Every link needs its own reason. Skipping the middle angles loses marks.',14),
P('p-perp-perp-par','par','Two lines perpendicular to the same transversal are parallel',
 'ℓ ⟂ t and m ⟂ t, with t cutting ℓ and m.','ℓ ∥ m',
 [('ℓ ⟂ t and m ⟂ t','Given'),('∠1 and ∠2 (corresponding angles) are right angles','Perpendicular lines form right angles'),('∠1 ≅ ∠2','All right angles are congruent'),('ℓ ∥ m','If corresponding angles are congruent, the lines are parallel')],
 'Right angles are all equal, so the corresponding angles match.',
 'This works in a plane. In 3D, two lines perpendicular to the same line can be skew.',
 'State that the lines are in the same plane when you use this theorem on a solid figure.',12),
P('p-trans-par','par','Two lines parallel to a third line are parallel to each other',
 'ℓ ∥ n and m ∥ n, with a transversal t crossing all three.','ℓ ∥ m',
 [('ℓ ∥ n','Given'),('∠1 ≅ ∠2 (corresponding, ℓ and n)','Corresponding angles postulate'),('m ∥ n','Given'),('∠3 ≅ ∠2 (corresponding, m and n)','Corresponding angles postulate'),('∠1 ≅ ∠3','Transitive property of congruence'),('ℓ ∥ m','If corresponding angles are congruent, the lines are parallel')],
 'Use n as a bridge: both ℓ and m match the same angle on n.',
 'This is the transitive property of parallelism. In the plane, parallel is an equivalence relation.',
 'The transversal must cross all three lines. Draw it before labelling angles.',15),
P('p-quad360','par','The angles of a quadrilateral add to 360°',
 'Quadrilateral ABCD with diagonal AC.','m∠A + m∠B + m∠C + m∠D = 360°',
 [('Draw diagonal AC, splitting ABCD into △ABC and △ACD','Two points determine a line'),('Angles of △ABC sum to 180°','Triangle angle sum theorem'),('Angles of △ACD sum to 180°','Triangle angle sum theorem'),('∠A and ∠C of the quadrilateral each equal the two pieces from the triangles','Angle addition postulate'),('m∠A + m∠B + m∠C + m∠D = 180° + 180° = 360°','Addition property of equality')],
 'Cut the shape into triangles you already know.',
 'Cutting an n-gon from one vertex gives n − 2 triangles, so the sum is (n − 2)·180°. This proof generalises directly.',
 'The diagonal must stay inside the quadrilateral. In a concave shape pick the diagonal from the reflex vertex.',13),
P('p-equi-equiang','tri','An equilateral triangle has three 60° angles',
 '△ABC with AB = BC = CA.','m∠A = m∠B = m∠C = 60°',
 [('AB = AC, so ∠B ≅ ∠C','Isosceles triangle theorem (base angles)'),('AB = BC, so ∠A ≅ ∠C','Isosceles triangle theorem (base angles)'),('∠A ≅ ∠B ≅ ∠C','Transitive property of congruence'),('m∠A + m∠B + m∠C = 180°','Triangle angle sum theorem'),('3·m∠A = 180°, so m∠A = 60°','Substitution and division property of equality')],
 'Use the isosceles theorem twice, then the angle sum.',
 'This is why every equilateral triangle is also equiangular. The converse is another proof (see the equiangular proof).',
 'A single isosceles step only equates two angles. You need a second pair to get all three.',12),
P('p-equiang-equi','tri','An equiangular triangle is equilateral',
 '△ABC with ∠A ≅ ∠B ≅ ∠C.','AB = BC = CA',
 [('∠A ≅ ∠B','Given'),('BC = AC','Converse of the isosceles triangle theorem (sides opposite congruent angles)'),('∠B ≅ ∠C','Given'),('AB = AC','Converse of the isosceles triangle theorem'),('AB = BC = CA','Transitive property of equality')],
 'Use the converse of the isosceles theorem for two different pairs of angles.',
 'Equiangular and equilateral are the same property for triangles, but NOT for other polygons (a rectangle is equiangular but not equilateral).',
 'The side opposite an angle is the side that does not touch it. Check which side you name.',13),
P('p-perpbis','tri','A point on the perpendicular bisector is equidistant from the endpoints',
 'ℓ is the perpendicular bisector of AB, meeting AB at M. P is on ℓ.','PA = PB',
 [('AM = MB','Definition of midpoint (bisector)'),('∠PMA ≅ ∠PMB, both right angles','Definition of perpendicular; all right angles are congruent'),('PM = PM','Reflexive property'),('△PMA ≅ △PMB','SAS'),('PA = PB','CPCTC')],
 'Make two triangles that share the segment PM.',
 'This theorem is the reason that the circumcentre (where perpendicular bisectors meet) is equally far from all three vertices.',
 'If P is on AB itself, then P = M and there are no triangles. Choose P off the line AB.',14),
P('p-perpbis-conv','tri','A point equidistant from A and B lies on the perpendicular bisector of AB',
 'PA = PB. M is the midpoint of AB.','PM ⟂ AB',
 [('PA = PB','Given'),('AM = MB','Definition of midpoint'),('PM = PM','Reflexive property'),('△PAM ≅ △PBM','SSS'),('∠PMA ≅ ∠PMB','CPCTC'),('∠PMA and ∠PMB are a linear pair and are congruent, so both are 90°','Congruent linear pair is a right angle'),('PM ⟂ AB','Definition of perpendicular')],
 'Use all three pairs of sides (SSS), then show the angles at M are equal and add to 180°.',
 'Together with the previous proof this says: the perpendicular bisector of AB is EXACTLY the set of points equidistant from A and B.',
 'Do not prove the first direction and claim both. A theorem and its converse need separate proofs.',15),
P('p-angbis-dist','tri','A point on an angle bisector is equidistant from the sides',
 'Ray BD bisects ∠ABC. P is on BD; PE ⟂ BA and PF ⟂ BC.','PE = PF',
 [('∠EBP ≅ ∠FBP','Definition of angle bisector'),('∠BEP ≅ ∠BFP, both right angles','Definition of perpendicular'),('BP = BP','Reflexive property'),('△BEP ≅ △BFP','AAS'),('PE = PF','CPCTC')],
 'Drop the perpendiculars to make two right triangles that share the bisector as hypotenuse.',
 'This is why the incentre (where angle bisectors meet) is the centre of the circle that touches all three sides.',
 'Distance to a line is the perpendicular distance, not the distance to some other point on the line.',14),
P('p-angbis-conv','tri','A point equidistant from the sides of an angle lies on its bisector',
 'PE ⟂ BA, PF ⟂ BC, and PE = PF.','Ray BP bisects ∠ABC',
 [('PE = PF','Given'),('∠BEP and ∠BFP are right angles','Definition of perpendicular'),('BP = BP','Reflexive property'),('△BEP ≅ △BFP','HL (Hypotenuse–Leg)'),('∠EBP ≅ ∠FBP','CPCTC'),('Ray BP bisects ∠ABC','Definition of angle bisector')],
 'Use HL, because you know right angles, one leg pair and a shared hypotenuse.',
 'AAS cannot be used here because the angles at B are what you are trying to show equal.',
 'Do not use SSA. HL is allowed only because the triangles are right triangles.',15),
P('p-tri-ineq','tri','The triangle inequality: AB + BC > AC',
 '△ABC. Extend AB past B to D so that BD = BC.','AB + BC > AC',
 [('BD = BC','Construction'),('AD = AB + BD','Segment addition postulate'),('AD = AB + BC','Substitution'),('∠BCD ≅ ∠D','Isosceles triangle theorem (BD = BC)'),('m∠ACD > m∠BCD','Whole is greater than its part (angle addition)'),('m∠ACD > m∠D','Substitution'),('AD > AC','Larger angle is opposite the longer side'),('AB + BC > AC','Substitution')],
 'Lay BC along the extension of AB so that AB + BC becomes a single segment, then compare it with AC.',
 'The inequality says a straight path is shortest. It also gives the test: three lengths form a triangle only when the longest is less than the sum of the others.',
 'The test must hold for EVERY pair, but checking that the longest side is less than the other two added is enough.',17),
P('p-hyp-long','rt','The hypotenuse is the longest side of a right triangle',
 '△ABC with a right angle at C.','AB > AC and AB > BC',
 [('m∠C = 90°','Given'),('m∠A + m∠B = 90°','Triangle angle sum (180° − 90°)'),('m∠A < 90° and m∠B < 90°','Each is positive and the two add to 90°'),('m∠C > m∠A and m∠C > m∠B','Substitution'),('AB > BC and AB > AC','Larger angle is opposite the longer side')],
 'Compare the right angle with the other two angles, then use “bigger angle faces bigger side”.',
 'This also follows from a² + b² = c², but the angle argument works without algebra.',
 'Do not argue from the picture. Use the angle-side relationship.',13),
P('p-ll','tri','Leg–Leg (LL) congruence for right triangles',
 'Right triangles ABC and DEF with right angles at C and F, AC = DF and BC = EF.','△ABC ≅ △DEF',
 [('AC = DF','Given'),('∠C ≅ ∠F, both right angles','Given; all right angles are congruent'),('BC = EF','Given'),('△ABC ≅ △DEF','SAS')],
 'The right angle sits between the two legs, so this is just SAS.',
 'LL, LA, HA and HL are all shortcuts for right triangles. LL is SAS, LA is ASA or AAS, HA is AAS, and HL is its own theorem.',
 'The right angle must be between the legs for SAS. It is, because legs meet at the right angle.',11),
P('p-kite-diag','quad','A kite’s diagonal bisects the angles at the vertices where equal sides meet',
 'Kite ABCD with AB = AD and CB = CD.','AC bisects ∠BAD and ∠BCD',
 [('AB = AD and CB = CD','Given'),('AC = AC','Reflexive property'),('△ABC ≅ △ADC','SSS'),('∠BAC ≅ ∠DAC and ∠BCA ≅ ∠DCA','CPCTC'),('AC bisects ∠BAD and ∠BCD','Definition of angle bisector')],
 'The diagonal splits the kite into two triangles that are mirror images.',
 'The mirror line is AC. That symmetry also gives BD ⟂ AC, which you can prove with the isosceles triangle ABD.',
 'The other diagonal BD does not bisect the angles at B and D unless the kite is a rhombus.',13),
P('p-kite-perp','quad','The diagonals of a kite are perpendicular',
 'Kite ABCD with AB = AD and CB = CD. Diagonals meet at E.','AC ⟂ BD',
 [('△ABC ≅ △ADC','SSS'),('∠BAE ≅ ∠DAE','CPCTC'),('AB = AD and AE = AE','Given; reflexive property'),('△ABE ≅ △ADE','SAS'),('∠AEB ≅ ∠AED','CPCTC'),('∠AEB and ∠AED are a congruent linear pair, so each is 90°','Linear pair postulate'),('AC ⟂ BD','Definition of perpendicular')],
 'First get the angles at A equal, then use them in a second pair of triangles.',
 'Here a second congruence is built on the first. This “two-stage” pattern appears in many harder proofs.',
 'Do not assume E is the midpoint of BD at the start. You can show it later with CPCTC.',16),
P('p-par-conv-opp','quad','A quadrilateral with both pairs of opposite sides equal is a parallelogram',
 'ABCD with AB = CD and AD = BC. Draw diagonal AC.','ABCD is a parallelogram',
 [('AB = CD and AD = BC','Given'),('AC = AC','Reflexive property'),('△ABC ≅ △CDA','SSS'),('∠BAC ≅ ∠DCA','CPCTC'),('AB ∥ CD','If alternate interior angles are congruent, the lines are parallel'),('∠BCA ≅ ∠DAC','CPCTC'),('AD ∥ BC','If alternate interior angles are congruent, the lines are parallel'),('ABCD is a parallelogram','Definition of parallelogram')],
 'Split with a diagonal, prove the triangles congruent, then turn equal angles into parallel lines.',
 'The converse of the parallelogram theorem. Together with its original, equal opposite sides is a complete characterisation.',
 'Show BOTH pairs of sides parallel. One pair is a trapezoid, not necessarily a parallelogram.',16),
P('p-diag-conv','quad','If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, it is a parallelogram',
 'Diagonals AC and BD of ABCD meet at E with AE = EC and BE = ED.','ABCD is a parallelogram',
 [('AE = EC and BE = ED','Given'),('∠AEB ≅ ∠CED','Vertical angles are congruent'),('△AEB ≅ △CED','SAS'),('∠BAE ≅ ∠DCE','CPCTC'),('AB ∥ CD','If alternate interior angles are congruent, the lines are parallel'),('AB = CD','CPCTC'),('ABCD is a parallelogram','If one pair of opposite sides is both parallel and congruent, the quadrilateral is a parallelogram')],
 'The diagonals give a pair of triangles through vertical angles. Prove one pair of sides parallel and equal.',
 'This shortcut needs only one pair of sides: parallel AND congruent.',
 'One pair of sides being just parallel, or just congruent, is not enough.',15),
P('p-rect-diag','quad','A parallelogram with equal diagonals is a rectangle',
 'Parallelogram ABCD with AC = BD.','ABCD is a rectangle',
 [('AB = DC','Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent'),('AC = DB','Given'),('BC = BC','Reflexive property'),('△ABC ≅ △DCB','SSS'),('∠ABC ≅ ∠DCB','CPCTC'),('∠ABC + ∠DCB = 180°','Same-side interior angles with AB ∥ DC'),('m∠ABC = 90°','Congruent and supplementary angles are 90° each'),('ABCD is a rectangle','A parallelogram with a right angle is a rectangle')],
 'Equal diagonals make two triangles congruent. Their matching angles are both equal and supplementary.',
 'In a parallelogram, consecutive angles are supplementary. Equal AND supplementary forces 90°.',
 'It is not enough to say the diagonals are equal in a general quadrilateral. The parallelogram is given.',16),
P('p-iso-trap','quad','Base angles of an isosceles trapezoid are congruent',
 'ABCD with AB ∥ CD and AD = BC. Draw AE ⟂ CD and BF ⟂ CD with E, F on CD.','∠D ≅ ∠C',
 [('AE ⟂ CD and BF ⟂ CD','Construction'),('AE ∥ BF','Lines perpendicular to the same line are parallel'),('AEFB is a parallelogram (AB ∥ EF)','Definition of parallelogram'),('AE = BF','Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent'),('△AED ≅ △BFC','HL (AD = BC, AE = BF)'),('∠D ≅ ∠C','CPCTC')],
 'Drop two perpendiculars to build right triangles you can prove congruent.',
 'The same proof shows the diagonals of an isosceles trapezoid are equal as well.',
 'HL needs right triangles, which is why the perpendiculars are built first.',16),
P('p-varignon','quad','The midpoints of any quadrilateral’s sides form a parallelogram',
 'E, F, G, H are the midpoints of AB, BC, CD, DA of quadrilateral ABCD.','EFGH is a parallelogram',
 [('Draw diagonal AC','Two points determine a line'),('EF ∥ AC and EF = ½·AC','Midsegment theorem in △ABC'),('HG ∥ AC and HG = ½·AC','Midsegment theorem in △ADC'),('EF ∥ HG and EF = HG','Transitive property of parallel lines and equality'),('EFGH is a parallelogram','One pair of opposite sides both parallel and congruent')],
 'Draw one diagonal and use the midsegment theorem on both triangles it makes.',
 'It works even if ABCD is concave or twisted. It is surprising that so little structure gives a parallelogram.',
 'You need both parallel AND equal for the same pair of sides.',17),
]
